1. Opening framing

Suggested opening remarks

“Today I want to position the Unstructured Data workstream as an enterprise architecture and capability-shaping initiative, not as a single project or platform build.

The problem we are trying to solve is that unstructured and semi-structured data is already being used across multiple AI use cases, but the delivery patterns are fragmented. Different teams are solving ingestion, preparation, semantic processing, storage, and retrieval locally, often with different controls and different assumptions.

This workstream exists to define the reusable enterprise model for how unstructured data should be onboarded, processed, governed, stored, and consumed across the bank.”

⸻

2. Why this workstream exists

Key message

“This workstream exists because unstructured data is becoming a foundational input into enterprise AI, but the bank does not yet have a consistent platform-centric operating model for it.”

Talk track

* Structured data already has a more mature platform and product model.
* Unstructured data is different because it introduces:
    * multiple modalities
    * more complex preparation
    * semantic processing
    * vector and graph patterns
    * stronger runtime governance needs
* Without a shared architecture and shared capability model, each use case will keep building its own local pattern.
* That creates:
    * duplicated cost
    * inconsistent controls
    * weaker auditability
    * slower onboarding of future use cases
    * and a growing divergence between structured and unstructured estates

One-line summary

“The workstream is about turning unstructured AI delivery from fragmented use-case implementation into a reusable, governed enterprise capability.”

⸻

3. Business value of the workstream

Main business value themes

I would present the value in four simple buckets.

1. Reuse and reduced duplication

“Instead of each use case building its own ingestion, preparation, chunking, embedding, and retrieval pattern, the bank can progressively shape reusable capabilities and shared services.”

2. Better governance and regulatory defensibility

“Unstructured AI introduces risks around sensitive data, provenance, lineage, runtime access, and AI-generated outputs. A shared architecture gives us stronger control points and more consistent evidence.”

3. Faster onboarding of future use cases

“If the capability model is defined once and adopted repeatedly, future use cases can onboard faster instead of starting from scratch.”

4. Better alignment between Data Platforms and Group AI

“This workstream provides a cleaner operating boundary: data platforms own platform-local execution and persistence boundaries, while Group AI owns approved model capability and shared AI services.”

One-line summary

“The business value is not just technical reuse; it is lower fragmentation, stronger control, faster onboarding, and a clearer enterprise operating model for AI on unstructured data.”

