Slide: Reference Architecture Introduction

What to say

“This slide introduces the target architecture at a high level.

The important message is that this is not one product and not one central platform. It is a reference model for the full lifecycle of unstructured data: from source onboarding, to preparation, to semantic processing, to governed persistence, to retrieval and publication.

The reason this matters is that unstructured AI needs more than model access. It needs a controlled path for how content enters the bank’s AI processing landscape, how it is transformed, what gets stored, and how it is consumed.

So this architecture gives us the enterprise backbone for that lifecycle.”

Emphasise

* lifecycle model
* not a monolithic platform
* reusable building blocks
* governance across the lifecycle

⸻

Slide: OKRs

What to say

“The OKRs are intended to move the workstream from architecture definition into practical enterprise capability shaping.

The objective is not only to document a reference architecture. It is to identify the shared capabilities that matter most, assess the current gaps, and define a realistic path to validate and progressively deliver them.

So the OKRs should be read as:

* define the target model
* identify reusable capabilities
* prioritise what matters most
* prove the enterprise patterns through focused capability validation”

Emphasise

* not architecture for its own sake
* architecture into capability prioritisation
* link to delivery and roadmap

⸻

Slide: Deep Dive — Reference Architecture

What to say

“This slide shows the architecture in more detail.

The key point is that the architecture is intentionally layered because different responsibilities sit in different parts of the lifecycle.

For example:

* ingestion is the first enterprise control boundary
* preparation is where canonicalisation, metadata, classification, and redaction happen
* semantic processing is where chunking, embeddings, graph, and validation sit
* persistence is role-based, not one storage pattern
* consumption includes both direct retrieval and governed publication

The important leadership message here is that unstructured AI is not just a model problem. It is a data architecture and control problem as well.”

Emphasise

* role separation across layers
* cross-cutting controls
* dual access model
* architecture as operating model

⸻

