Slide: VectorDBaaS

What to say

“This slide zooms into one priority shared service: VectorDBaaS.

The important point is that VectorDBaaS is not the workstream. It is one priority shared service within the wider target model.

Why it matters is that vector capability is emerging in multiple AI use cases, and without an enterprise service model we will end up with fragmented local vector stores, inconsistent controls, and duplicated platform effort.”

Core explanation

“VectorDBaaS gives the bank a governed service model for vector persistence and retrieval:

* central control plane for standards, lifecycle, contracts, and governance
* federated data planes aligned to each data platform
* vectors stay local to platform and residency boundaries
* approved embeddings are consumed through Group AI
* direct runtime retrieval and governed publication can both be supported”

Critical distinction to make

“Group AI owns models. VectorDBaaS does not own models.
VectorDBaaS owns the governed vector service layer.”

Leadership value message

“This is how we separate model ownership from vector service ownership and avoid per-use-case vector sprawl.”

⸻

Slide: Codify the Bank reference use case

What to say

“This slide is important because it shows that the workstream is grounded in real delivery evidence.

Codify the Bank is a strong reference use case because it exercises many of the architectural patterns we are trying to shape:

* document ingestion
* preparation and normalisation
* deterministic and agentic workflow
* validation and HITL
* graph and vector coexistence
* governed retrieval

So this is not theoretical architecture. It is architecture shaped from real use-case evidence.”

Leadership message

“The role of the use case here is not to define the enterprise target on its own, but to validate that the target architecture is grounded and practical.”

